    "name": "Inverse Effectiveness in Multisensory Compensation",
    "structural_pattern": "COMPENSATION",
    "higher_order_principle": "Multisensory enhancement is strongest when individual unisensory signals are weak or degraded. A degraded sense (e.g., in dim light) is compensated for by increased weighting of other senses. Design for all senses is most critical in difficult conditions.",
